  doi = {10.1093/eurpub/ckaa001},
  urldate = {2024-12-04},
  abstract = {The world we live in is hugely unequal, not only in terms of income and wealth, but also in terms of life-span and health. What makes it worse is that the two are correlated: people who have less material resources also tend to die younger and to have more health problems within their shorter lives. Wide-spread concern with these health inequalities has over the past decades led to an impressive stream of increasingly sophisticated research to find explanations. The growth in scientific understanding has also inspired confidence that we can reduce health inequalities. Many policy proposals have been made, both nationally and internationally, culminating in the World Health Organization's report `Closing the gap in a generation'.1 Yet, progress in reducing health inequalities has been disappointing, and on some measures health inequalities have even widened over time. So the question is: what went wrong?},
